• The exact locations in `PRIOR_FINDINGS.txt` now contain the requested fixes: canonical repository validation, push-URL checks, `check` and bot guards, installer migration, and the named documentation updates. The following problems remain:

  - `scripts/land/lib.sh:91-102` — GitHub hostname matching is case-sensitive and does not normalize trailing-dot DNS names. URLs such as `git@GITHUB.COM:unbalancedparentheses/concrete2.git` or `git@github.com.:unbalancedparentheses/concrete2.git` bypass the check entirely, including the `LAND_REPO` check, and can still push to the archived repository. **Fix:** parse and normalize the hostname case-insensitively, including a trailing dot, before extracting the repository path. Add uppercase and trailing-dot stale-URL refusal tests.

  - `scripts/land/notes.sh:24-33,77`, `scripts/land/ready.sh:32-36`, `scripts/review/review.sh:48,90-113`, `scripts/review/verify.sh:19-26` — direct readiness and review workflows remain unguarded. `notes.sh put` and `review.sh` can push notes to a stale remote; `ready.sh` and `verify.sh --fetch` can read it. The bot’s guard does not protect these direct entry points. **Fix:** source `lib.sh` and run `land_remote_bad` before every fetch/push-enabled operation, using `REVIEW_REMOTE` where applicable. Add stale-remote tests proving no fetch or push occurs.

  - `scripts/land/install-bot.sh:64-83` — the generated service launcher executes `git fetch -q origin` at line 70 before entering the newly guarded `bot.sh`. A bot clone that later becomes stale therefore reads the archived repository before being refused. `scripts/checks/landing_bot.sh:407-418` invokes `bot.sh` directly, so its passing tests miss the actual service path. **Fix:** perform canonical fetch/push URL validation inside the generated launcher before its first fetch. Test the generated launcher with a stale origin and assert that no fetch occurs.

  - `scripts/land/gates_transport_ssh.sh:34`, `scripts/land/run-at-ref.sh:25-36` — the separate server gate clone fetches `origin` without validation. Nothing in the installer repoints this `~/concrete2` clone, despite `docs/LANDING.md:221` asserting that its origin is canonical. An existing stale gate clone will still read the archived repository. **Fix:** validate the gate clone’s effective origin URLs on the host before fetching, and migrate or explicitly refuse stale gate clones. Add a stale-gate-clone control.

  - `scripts/land/train.sh:699-758,799-849` — `watch` and `record` call GitHub through `$REPO` without `require_remote_repo`. With an old `LAND_REPO=unbalancedparentheses/concrete2`, these commands still read the archived repository even though `build`, `check`, `ci`, and `land` refuse it. **Fix:** call `require_remote_repo` before the first GitHub operation in both commands and add tests asserting the fake `gh` is never invoked for the old-origin/old-environment combination.

  - `scripts/land/acceptance.sh:91-94,189-195` — acceptance clones the source repository before any canonical-remote validation. A stale source is therefore read even though the subsequent train build refuses it. **Fix:** validate every effective fetch and push URL of the source `origin` before `git clone`; add a refusal test proving no clone is attempted.

  - `ROADMAP.md:519` — the active R-0563 acceptance criterion still requires a “two-remote landing.” `docs/LANDING.md:188-192` and `changelog.d/2026-11-07-one-repository.md:12-14` also describe validation relative to overridable `LAND_REPO`, rather than the canonical constant, and omit `check` from the guarded commands. **Fix:** update these to the single-main landing and canonical-repository behavior.

  Read-only verification: all changed shell scripts passed `bash -n`. I did not run the test suites because they create repositories and other state.

